WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.420
Hey youtubers it’s Charlie and the first
episode of Rebels just posted, so this will

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.840
be my non spoilery review.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:14.160
I’m expecting that most of you won’t have
seen it yet, so when they air it on tv I’ll

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.570
do a top 10 video with spoilers next week.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:24.560
I will be doing weekly videos for all the
episodes, so if you like talking about star

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.210
wars please subscribe to get everything.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.900
It’s an amazing show, anyone who liked the
clone wars is going to love rebels.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.809
The episode is called Spark of Rebellion and
it introduces all the big heroes and villains

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:38.649
along with explaining the state of the galaxy.

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:41.430
The series takes place 5 years before Episode
4.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.920
So presumably when they end the series.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.539
It will be just as Princess Leia is getting
chased down by the star destroyer in the opening

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:49.060
moments of the movie.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.760
The cast has a lot of familiar faces and the
production crew is the exact same group of

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.149
people that did Clone Wars.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:58.510
Simon Kinberg is also writing for the series.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.079
He’s one of the people working on the new
Star Wars movies.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:09.010
So there’s a lot of overlap between tv show
people and movie people.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:14.920
And you can see it in the writing.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:22.190
I really want to talk about the voice cast
first.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:28.160
The most recognizable people are Steven Blum,
Vanessa Marshall and Freddie Prinze Jr., if

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.900
you’re a big fan of Young Justice, Legend
of Korra, Cowboy Bebop or you were a human

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.310
being in the 90s who watched movies.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.210
You will instantly recognize those voices.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:43.050
Jason Isaacs character we only meet briefly,
so I’m not going to talk as much about him

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.710
until he gets some more screen time.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.710
Even if you haven’t seen the episode, you
could look at these people and assume it’s

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.900
going to be good.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:55.000
Thankfully that is the case.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.330
Freddie Prinze Jr was a ton of fun as the
main character, he’s filling the Obi-Wan

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:04.040
Role, he’ll be mentoring slash training
Ezra, played by Taylor Gray when their team

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.299
is in between missions.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:09.200
They’re like a loveable band of rogues that
are part scoundrel part hero.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.520
They’d be boring if they never broke the
law.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:23.410
Freddie Prinze gets this 
really iconic Obi-Wan Kenobi speech that I

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:31.140
think serves as a good test for him as a voice
actor.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:43.350
I think 

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:49.400
based on the characters he’s played before,
being the loveable scoundrel will come a lot

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.230
easier than playing the jedi master.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.210
I thought he nailed it and I’m looking forward
to more Jedi - Padawan moments.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:00.120
It’s really easy for someone to sound silly
while they’re trying to speechify in jedi

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.120
mode.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.120
Alec Guiness for instance, was totally believable
as a Jedi Master, you took him very seriously.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.120
And were still able to chuckle when he made
a subtle turn of phrase.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.880
The rest of the voice cast didn’t get quite
as much screen time, but people like Steve

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.640
Blum and the other really notable people I
mentioned are always amazing

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.920
Most of the time when we’re watching the
show I think we’ll be focusing on the star

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.920
wars specific things, like the plot, the sights,
the sounds and the giant Death Star looming

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.920
in the distance.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.341
Speaking of the stories, you guys will be
happy to know that Rebels will be canon, so

00:03:21.341 --> 00:03:24.540
things that happen on the show will affect
events during the movies.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.910
And you don’t have to have watched Clone
Wars to understand what’s going on, but

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:32.260
there is a very notable appearance from James
Taylor as Obi Wan Kenobi.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:39.110
The Disney people said that they officially
consider Clone Wars as canon.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.530
Maybe not a 100 percent, but for all intents
and purposes Ashoka and everyone else are

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.900
canon.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:56.650
I don’t know if they have any plans to include
those characters in Rebels, but they included

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.490
Obi-Wan, so maybe.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:09.820
The state of the galaxy is a big deal in the
episode.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.620
This is a time period we haven’t seen yet
but it’s instantly recognizable even if

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.620
you haven’t been watching the cartoons.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.979
The animation bears some similarities to the
computer animation style of Clone Wars, but

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:24.090
all the textures look like they’re cranked
to 11.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.120
I don’t know if it’s just because the
animation software has gotten more sophisticated

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:34.490
or if it was just a creative decision but
everything looks much more steampunk-y and

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:35.870
Episode 4-ish.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.180
Like all the lines are clean and everything
pops with amazing color but it still bears

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.139
a lot of visual influence from the original
trilogy.

00:04:44.139 --> 00:05:05.449
I think this is because of the new films and
JJ Abrams decisions to go with a more classic

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:06.449
visual style.

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:12.830
I say classic just to differentiate the visual
style of the original trilogy with the prequels.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:20.850
The episode itself structures most of it’s
biggest scenes around the big WTF moments

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.969
of the original trilogy.

00:05:22.969 --> 00:05:28.280
Like we see a really bad ass speeder bike
chase that hits all the same beats of the

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.250
one in Episode 6 and we see a very familiar
prison block scene.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:37.020
Even the hyperspace sequences look very similar
to the ones in the movies.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.720
Part of me feels like they turned this into
a love letter to the original trilogy because

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:49.870
the love it and part of me feels like they’re
doing it to prepare us for the visual style

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:57.180
of the JJ Abrams Star Wars Universe.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.960
If you haven’t seen the Episode 7 supposed
Storm Trooper helmets, they look like they

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.830
could be future versions of the helmets we
see in the episode.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.069
So it seems like they’re making minor adjustments
to things so that it all fits together logically

00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:10.710
in the canon.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:15.479
So when you see the episode, just be on the
look out for big movie moments done with the

00:06:15.479 --> 00:06:16.479
tv show characters.

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:17.529
They’re all really easy to spot.

00:06:17.529 --> 00:06:20.990
There’s a certain amount of subtext but
the show is written so that a young child

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.430
could follow the broad themes.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.529
Older fans will enjoy a few well placed willhelm
screams.

00:06:25.529 --> 00:06:28.169
And that’s another thing I really noticed.

00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:32.169
None of the dialogue or ‘star wars specific’
sound effects tripped over each other.

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:35.229
Everything was very distinct, I could follow
all the dialogue and the sound effects at

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:36.229
the same time.

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:40.240
It’s just a Star Wars geek thing I’ve
always done since before the Prequels.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.749
It’s like a game, “where did this sound
effect first appear”.

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:45.729
Or which scene did this noise come from?

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:48.610
If you really want to be hardcore about it,
you’ll have a lot of fun.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:53.180
There are also a lot of familiar music cues
from episode 4.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.849
The whole story structure of the episode mostly
takes from episode 4, but there are a few

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.689
epic Empire Strikes back and Jedi moments.

00:06:58.689 --> 00:07:03.849
I think they only thing I have to complain
about is that one, it’s too short.

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:09.710
I understand the demands of animation make
half hour shows less cumbersome to produce,

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:10.710
and two.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.210
The Disney XD app and website where you can
watch this for free, doesn’t let you watch

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.270
it unless you get Disney XD as part of your
cable tv subscription.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:21.639
That’s not the bad part, not all the cable
providers they list on their website are part

00:07:21.639 --> 00:07:22.639
of the deal.

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:24.880
So if you have Time Warner, you can’t watch
this.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.719
Even if you pay for the Disney XD channel.

00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:33.249
That’s really the only actual complaint.

00:07:33.249 --> 00:07:39.190
The episode will be on television October
3rd if you have to wait.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:41.710
But like I said, you can watch it right now,
so don’t wait.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:46.499
Since that’s next friday my next Rebels
video will be my top 10 favorite moments from

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:47.669
the episode with spoilers.

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:52.620
And after Friday, On October 13th the show
moves to Monday Nights at 9PM eastern time.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.150
What I’m going to do is because Gotham airs
before it, I’ll be posting Gotham videos

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.680
Monday Night and my Rebels videos Tuesday
Morning.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.131
I’ll still try to live tweet as much as
possible while it’s on tv, if you want to

00:08:03.131 --> 00:08:06.319
play along you can find all my social links
down in the description.

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:11.230
I’m also pretty good about giving updates
on when videos are coming on twitter and Facebook.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.150
I’m putting all my Rebels videos in my big
